so the nobel prize for literature has  just been awarded to a tanzanian-born  novelist  guerner's work focuses on colonialism  and the fate of refugees he arrived in  the uk  as a refugee age 18 in the 1960s  and has now retired from teaching  english at the university of kent let's  go to paul reese who's now  in stockholm so paul how much of a  landmark is this year's prize  i think very much a landmark nick  especially for african literature  obviously for abdul razak gurner i'm  trying not to be impolite because the uh  press conference is still going on uh  behind me uh guerner the recipient of  this prize after  a process that's seen countless  nominations coming from around the world  sifted down to a list of five by the  nobel committee a secret list of five  we'll never know we will know in 50  years who the other nominees were and  then the chairman of the nobel committee  coming through the doors behind me and  making the rather solemn announcement  about  10 minutes ago but this is is  significant for  for one reason a few years ago the  swedish academy nearly 250 years old  institution  promised to modernize especially in the  wake of a me too scandal here in  stockholm involving the academy promised  to be more gender balanced promised to  be more global and  they have fulfilled that letter now this  is the first prize to a non-european or  american  in nearly a decade  but perhaps one stark statistic still is  that only 16 women have won this prize  still out of the 118  winners overall so 102 men to 16 women  um but still with abdul razak gerner's  prize cited for uh his writings on the  effect of colonialism um  giving a voice to refugees this is  obviously a wonderful day for african  literature  all right paul thanks very much uh  maurice in stockholm
 

